; The phase-2 choice fixture: one shape per row the choice projection has to render, and nothing
; else. A choice becomes a `resource` with NO constructor — there is nothing to build without
; picking an arm — so the `new-<variant>` statics replace it, `kind` reports which arm is live and
; `as-<variant>` hands the payload back.

; a bounded newtype, so a choice arm can name a class-backed type
stamp = bytes .size 4

; a TYPE choice covering four arm classes at once: a VALUE-BOUNDED primitive (its rust `new_uint`
; is fallible, so the static unwraps rather than wraps), a plain primitive, a named class-backed arm
; (crossing as a `borrow<stamp>` in and an owned `stamp` out), and a FIXED-value arm — which gets a
; `new-null` taking nothing and NO `as-null`, because it carries no payload for one to return
outcome = uint .le 5 / text / stamp / null

; a type choice whose arm is a DESPECIALIZED collection: `[+ text]` is type-enforced in rust and a
; plain `list<string>` in WIT, so `new-labels` is fallible for a reason the rust constructor knows
; nothing about — the boundary has to re-enter the `TryFrom` door itself
labels = [+ text]
label = labels / uint

; a GROUP choice, array rep, covering the three arm shapes the rust enum spells differently: an
; INLINED single-field arm (the payload rides the variant), a multi-field arm (which mints its own
; struct and whose `new-` takes that struct's fields directly, exactly as the rust ctor does), and a
; fixed-only arm
node = [ 0, leaf: stamp // 1, x: uint, y: text // 2 ]

; a record holding each of them — the handle direction — plus a choice inside a collection, which is
; what makes the reference closure and the element-wise handle minting non-trivial
holder = {
  o: outcome,
  l: label,
  n: node,
  history: [* outcome],
}
